Osza Browser - Changelog
========================

Unreleased
---------
The visual refresh: one icon language everywhere. Security tools: the
start of the bundled security suite.

Added:
- The living address bar: page loads are eased, never jumped. The
  engine reports load progress in bursts (10, 26, 43, 76, ...), and
  the bar painted whatever number last arrived - a staircase - and
  then vanished the moment a 700 ms timer fired. The progress
  treatment now tells one continuous story: a variant animation
  eases the displayed value toward every report (OutCubic, 320 ms,
  each retarget starting from where the display stands, so a burst
  mid-glide redirects the same motion instead of cutting to a new
  one), the bar sweeps to 100 as the load finishes, dwells there
  briefly, and eases its opacity out (300 ms) rather than dropping
  - and a fresh navigation resets everything from zero, wherever
  the previous story stood. The new default style is the accent
  line: a rounded line riding the bottom edge of the address pill,
  its caps kept clear of the pill's curvature, a soft glow rising
  off it, a brighter tip leading the motion with a small bloom,
  and a whisper fill over the loaded region clipped to the pill's
  shape. The accent needs no theme-specific code: it follows the
  palette's Highlight role, which the Cyber theme's #locationbar
  rule already paints mint (#00ffd1), the private variant violet
  (#a78bfa), and the system palette its own accent - and the
  custom color set in Preferences still wins over all of it. The
  classic Fill, Bottom and Top styles stay, now eased and fading
  like the line; the thin edge lines ride the same accent (they
  never cover text), while Fill keeps its calm text-mid tone
  because it paints over the text. Right-to-left layouts now read
  every style anchored to the edge the text grows from - the old
  code filled from the left edge regardless. Out of the box the
  feature is on: ShowLoadingProgress defaults to true and
  ProgressStyle to the accent line (the classic indices are
  stable, so a stored choice keeps its meaning), and the
  Preferences selector gains "Accent line" as its fourth entry.
  Verified offline by the new tools/ui/verify_load_progress.py -
  the one-writer discipline on the eased value, the easing
  contract of both animations, the lifecycle invariants (only the
  fade's finish retires the bar), the stable style order, the
  treatment's drawing invariants, the accent resolver, the RTL
  mirrors, and the three default readers agreeing - 45 checks.
- The complete Osza Cyber theme: the flagship dark design now
  covers the whole application, not just the chrome. A theme here
  is an application-wide stylesheet and nothing else - the
  application palette still follows the system - so every widget
  class the stylesheet does not mention renders in the system
  look. The Cyber theme styled the main window (tabs, toolbar,
  location and search pills, menus, statusbar) and stopped there:
  every dialog - Preferences, home of the entire security suite,
  Download Manager, Library, Site Info, the exception managers -
  opened as a light island inside the dark browser, its 123
  buttons, 99 checkboxes, 58 line edits, 24 combos, radios, spin
  boxes, group boxes, tab widgets, tree and list views, scroll
  bars and tooltips all drawn by the system. The theme now carries
  the design's surface ladder (#12141d panels, #0d0f16 fields and
  cards, #262b3a hairlines, #00ffd1 accent) across the whole
  inventory: dialogs and every top-level window that is not a
  QDialog (Download Manager, Library, the license viewer, popup
  windows, the location-bar completion and the padlock menu's
  popup family), buttons with a mint primary for the dialog's
  default action, dark fields with the cyan focus ring and mint
  selection, combos with styled popups, mint check and radio
  indicators (mirrored for the checkable rows inside item views),
  spin boxes with quiet chevron buttons, sliders, progress bars,
  item views with mint-tinted selection and styled headers and
  branch chevrons, dialog tab widgets whose selected tab echoes
  the chrome's cyan top edge, group boxes as quiet cards,
  separators, slim scroll bars, dark tooltips, and the JS alert
  frames, whose stock rules asked the light system palette for
  their colors and now join the design. Two stylesheet mechanics
  were leaned on deliberately: the chrome's hand-tuned ID
  selectors always outrank the new class rules, so the chrome
  keeps its exact geometry (the dialog tab rules are scoped inside
  QTabWidget so they can never reach the main tab strip, whose
  tabs are drawn through QTabBar style rules as well); and a
  stylesheet that styles a background recolors the palette the
  widget hands to custom painters, so the location-bar completion
  rows, the downloads rows and the site-info rows follow the dark
  surfaces without any code change. The private-mode variant
  restates every accent-driven rule in violet, and the RTL sheet
  mirrors the new tree-branch chevron. Nine small indicator
  artworks join the theme's image set (the check glyph, combo and
  spin chevrons, tree branch arrows, the tool button menu arrow),
  drawn in the icon language of the bundled set.
- Theme verification: tools/themes/verify_theme.py, the theme
  completeness verifier, in the family of the offline security
  verifiers. Themes come in two families: palette-following ones
  (linux, windows, mac, chrome) whose unstyled widgets inherit the
  system look by design, and self-contained designs (osza-cyber)
  that hardcode their surfaces and must therefore cover every
  widget class the browser instantiates. Without building Qt the
  tool checks, for every bundled theme: QSS sanity - balanced
  blocks, known properties, pseudo-states and subcontrols (an
  unknown pseudo-state silently drops its whole rule), url()
  artwork present on disk, no constructs Qt style sheets do not
  implement; for self-contained themes: widget coverage - every
  Qt widget class instantiated in the tree's .ui files (custom
  classes resolved through the source to their Qt base) that
  paints a visible surface must be mentioned by the theme's
  selectors, and every custom class the theme names must exist;
  and the ordering invariants the coverage relies on - the item
  view rules that carry [frameShape="6"] must follow the
  QFrame card rule they override by cascade order, and the
  WebView page background must exist. Its first run found two
  real bugs in the chrome theme that had been shipping silently
  (see Fixed), and it keeps the coverage honest as widgets are
  added to the tree.
- Security tools (24): the interstitial re-serves itself when a
  refresh lands - the evidence re-weighs itself. A blocklist
  refresh completing while the warning page stood changed nothing
  on the page: the list-state row kept saying "updating now"
  against a list that had already landed, the movement the
  refresh brought went unspoken, and a site the new list no
  longer flagged kept standing behind a warning its own evidence
  no longer supported - the only cue that a reload would now say
  something new was the location bar's pulse stopping, an
  invitation to notice the chrome and press reload by hand. The
  page now renews itself: every page showing the standing warning
  (tab or popup - every page in the browser is a WebPage) wires
  the manager's two lifecycle ends, a list landing and a failure,
  to a guarded reload of its own osza:threatblocked address, and
  the scheme handler answers with the evidence re-weighed against
  the list now in place - the fresh size, age and movement, the
  entry that now matches (or the honesty of "not on the current
  blocklist"), or the named failure with its retry. The reload
  re-runs the page build, never the block decision: an osza:
  address passes the interceptor by, exactly like the original
  serve, so the re-serve can only make the page more honest, never
  less careful. The start of a refresh is deliberately unwired -
  it changes nothing the page can say (the location bar's pulse
  witnesses it), so a warning being read is not disturbed for a
  tail it will speak when the refresh ends - and the in-flight
  tail now tells the user the promise the wiring keeps ("updating
  now; this page renews itself when the update completes").
  Verified three ways. The extended offline
  tools/security/verify_threat_block_update_ux.py pins the wiring
  (both ends, never the start), the one-cheap-question guard on
  the page's own address, the single reload, and the in-flight
  note's wording; the new threatBlockInterstitialReServeTest
  drives the real WebEngine end to end - a refresh landing beside
  a standing warning (the re-served page speaks the movement and
  re-weighs the match), a list landing without the host (the
  match row answers honestly), a page that is not the warning
  staying exactly where it is, and a failed refresh re-serving
  the failure with its retry. The other six security verifiers
  still pass unchanged.
- Security tools (23): the movement joins the coverage row and the
  sidebar - one composed movement, every surface that speaks the
  age. The warning page's evidence row alone spoke the blocklist's
  movement ("+1,204 hosts since the last refresh"); the padlock
  menu's coverage row and the security sidebar's status row spoke
  the age they speak ("list updated less than a minute ago", "3
  hosts, updated less than a minute ago") with no word of what the
  refresh had moved - so two surfaces answering the same question
  ("how fresh is this evidence?") gave two different answers, one
  naming the change and the others silent about it. Both rows now
  append the same composed parenthetical the evidence row rides:
  the wording is built in exactly one place
  (ThreatBlockManager::listMovementLocked, served to both rows by
  the public self-locking listMovement(), used by the evidence row
  under the one lock its snapshot already holds - the same split
  the session-allow query keeps), so the interstitial, the padlock
  menu and the sidebar can never speak two different movements for
  one refresh, and the silences travel with it: a first list ever
  downloaded, the same list reloaded from disk after a restart,
  and no list at all stay silent on every surface, never invented.
  The movement rides wherever the age is spoken - the padlock
  row's fresh state ("This site is not on the malicious-site
  blocklist. (list updated 3 hours ago) (+1,204 hosts since the
  last refresh)"), the sidebar's loaded state before its failure
  tail - and the rows re-render through the manager's own signals
  while a menu is open or the sidebar watches, so the movement a
  refresh just brought is spoken the moment it lands. Verified by
  the extended offline tools/security/verify_threat_block_update_
  ux.py (the one composition and its silences, the two wordings
  existing exactly once in the tree, both rows appending the query
  next to their age, and the interstitial composing through the
  private form under its lock), by the extended
  threatBlockListDeltaTest (the public query's wordings and
  silences pinned beside the page's) and by the new
  threatBlockListMovementSurfacesTest, which drives a real
  padlock menu and a real sidebar against the one string the
  evidence row speaks.
- Security tools (22): the blocklist's movement on the evidence
  rows - "+1,204 hosts since the last refresh". The warning page's
  "why is this site flagged?" disclosure named the list's size and
  its age, but not how it had moved: after "Refresh the blocklist
  now" (or the revoke of a "proceed anyway", which now renews as
  protection resumes) the row said "125,204 hosts, updated less
  than a minute ago" with no word of the 1,204 hosts the refresh
  had just added - so the size of the change in the evidence, the
  very thing a renewal was asked for, went unspoken. The list-state
  row now speaks the movement the last refresh brought, next to the
  size and age it belongs to: a growth with its sign ("+1,204 hosts
  since the last refresh"), a loss the same way ("-30 hosts since
  the last refresh"), a refresh that moved nothing as "(unchanged
  since the last refresh)" - which is itself evidence - and silence
  where no movement can honestly be claimed: the first list a
  profile ever downloads (nothing came before it), and the same
  list reloaded from disk after a restart (the movement was not
  seen, so it is not invented). The memory is one place and one
  judgment: applyListData() - the one path a renewal takes, never
  the startup disk load - remembers the count of the list it
  replaces against the count of the list landing, mutex-guarded
  like the rest of the update lifecycle and read by the evidence
  row under the one lock the whole snapshot already holds, so a
  row's size, age and movement can never describe two different
  refreshes. The movement is deliberately session memory, not
  persisted state: the blocklist file carries the list, and after a
  restart the row speaks what it can know. Verified by the extended
  offline tools/security/verify_threat_block_update_ux.py (the
  remembering site and its timing, the disk load's and applyList's
  silence, the wording contracts, and a Python mirror of the
  movement judgment with its silences) and by the new
  threatBlockListDeltaTest autotest, which drives every state
  through applyListData() - the first list, a growth, a loss, an
  unchanged refresh, and the restart's disk load - and pins the
  row's answer for each.
- Security tools (21): the blocklist update indicator in the
  location bar - the renewal is witnessed in the chrome. Every
  surface that spoke the blocklist's update lifecycle spoke it after
  the fact or behind a menu: the warning page said "updating now",
  the padlock row said "being refreshed now", the sidebar ticked -
  but the location bar, the one surface always in view, said nothing
  while a refresh ran, and the browser stayed silent about security
  work being done on the user's behalf. A refresh now pulses there:
  while one runs, the site icon (and the popup windows' location
  bars) breathes - a soft cyan halo behind the glyph, swelling and
  ebbing through a triangle-wave phase rather than blinking, the
  suite's calm voice where the amber badge speaks a standing
  exception and the warning page speaks red - and the tooltip names
  it ("The malicious-site blocklist is being refreshed now."). The
  pulse is one drawing code (SiteIcon::paintUpdatePulse), composited
  behind whatever icon the bar currently shows, so a standing
  exception's amber dot and a renewal in flight speak side by side,
  at the 16 px scale and the popup's larger one; the breath is
  stepped by an 80 ms timer through the one recomposition path every
  other icon change takes, and the tooltip is composed in one place
  so the two setters can never disagree about what the icon says.
  The wiring: a new ThreatBlockManager::updateStarted() completes
  the update lifecycle's signals - emitted exactly where an attempt
  actually begins (outside the mutex, so a slot may safely ask the
  state back; before the source is even weighed, so an attempt
  against an invalid source is still an announced attempt that
  updateFailed immediately ends) - and both location bars connect it
  with listUpdated and updateFailed to one slot that asks the same
  mutex-guarded isUpdating() every other surface reports through, so
  the pulse can never disagree with the rows beneath it. And the one
  moment the chrome most needs it: revoking a "proceed anyway"
  ("Block this site again" in the padlock menu) now renews the
  evidence it re-weighs with - the refresh starts beside the reload
  (never in test mode, the rule every update trigger follows), the
  re-served warning names it in flight, and the badge breathes until
  the list lands or the failure is named. Verified by the extended
  offline tools/security/verify_locationbar_badge.py (one drawing
  code for both marks and both surfaces, the breath's shape, the
  lifecycle wiring in both bars, the one tooltip composition, the
  no-op discipline) and by the new locationBarListUpdatePulseTest
  autotest, which drives the pulse end to end in a real window's
  location bar - the glow arriving with its tooltip, the breath
  moving, the icon returning to itself when the refresh ends - while
  the lifecycle test now pins updateStarted firing exactly once for
  an attempt that fails at once.
- Security tools (20): the blocklist update UX - the evidence keeps
  its freshness visible, and can be renewed in place. The
  interstitial's "why is this site flagged?" disclosure and the
  padlock menu's threat-block row both spoke of the list as a bare
  snapshot ("124,000 hosts, updated 14:32"), with no notion of a
  refresh in flight, no memory of a refresh that had failed, and no
  way to renew the evidence where it was being weighed - so a
  warning could be explained by a list hours or days old, and taken
  on faith. Both surfaces now speak the whole update lifecycle: the
  age in relative terms ("124,000 hosts, updated 3 hours ago" - the
  question a person weighing "proceed anyway" is actually asking),
  a refresh in flight ("updating now", no action offered while one
  runs), the last refresh's failure named next to the list it left
  behind ("the last update failed: ...", with the retry), and, when
  the list is due, the renewal itself - "Refresh the blocklist
  now" as a link in the warning page's evidence rows (an osza:
  address the scheme handler answers by starting the refresh and
  re-serving the same page, so the row then reports the refresh in
  flight; never in test mode, like every update trigger) and as a
  button in the padlock menu, which starts the refresh and stays
  open: the manager's own signals re-render the row while the menu
  is open, so the update is witnessed in the place that offered
  it, ending in either the fresh state or the named failure with
  the retry. The "due" judgment is spoken once: a new public
  ThreatBlockManager::isListStale() answers "is this list empty or
  older than the refresh interval" through the same file-local
  helper the manager's own timer has always refreshed through, so
  the timer can never fetch a list the surfaces still call fresh,
  nor leave alone one they already call due. The failure is
  remembered, not just announced: a new lastUpdateError() holds the
  last attempt's error (set before every updateFailed emission,
  cleared when a new attempt starts or a list lands), so a failed
  refresh stays visible long after the signal has come and gone -
  in the warning page, the padlock row, the security sidebar (whose
  live status now also speaks the relative age, ticking every
  second) and the self-test page's threat row, all in the same
  "%1 hosts, updated %2" voice; Preferences keeps the clock time,
  the configuration surface where the source is set. The age itself
  comes from one new static, describeListAge() ("less than a minute
  ago" / "N minute(s) ago" / "N hour(s) ago" / "N day(s) ago",
  clamped against clock skew), and the in-flight flag became
  mutex-guarded shared state, read under the one lock the
  interstitial already holds for its evidence rows (the page is
  rendered on the WebEngine IO thread). The padlock row's coverage
  states carry the age in parentheses ("This site is not on the
  malicious-site blocklist (list updated 3 hours ago).") with the
  renewal offered exactly when isListStale() says the list is due,
  the loading states distinguish a download in flight from a failed
  one, and the per-site actions keep their own button and their
  close-on-apply behavior. Verified by the new offline
  tools/security/verify_threat_block_update_ux.py (the staleness
  boundary, the age buckets, the remembering discipline, the one
  lock, the href escaping, the handler's test-mode rule and the
  autotest coverage, all without a Qt build) and by the new
  threatBlockListUpdateStateTest autotest, which drives every state
  without the network: a fresh list, a failed refresh through an
  invalid source, a recovery, and a stale list through a
  hand-written list file with an old "# updated:" header - the file
  a real profile carries after a long offline stretch.
- Security tools (19): the threat-block interstitial's "why is this
  site flagged?" details. The warning page said a blocklist was the
  reason, but not which entry of which list said so - someone
  weighing "proceed anyway", a forum helping them, or an admin
  auditing a fleet had to take the browser's word for it. The page
  now carries a "Why is this site flagged?" disclosure under the
  "Back to safety" button, and it opens on the evidence: the
  matching blocklist entry, named together with how it matched (the
  host itself is on the list, or the visited host is a subdomain of
  the listed entry that covers it), the blocklist source the
  decision was made against, and the list's size and last update
  (the same "%1 hosts, updated %2" wording the sidebar, Preferences
  and the self-test page already use, so one list state is spoken
  of in one voice everywhere). The disclosure is a native
  <details> on purpose: the toggle needs no script - keyboard and
  assistive tech get the semantics from the element itself - the
  built-in marker is silenced both ways for every WebEngine Osza
  runs on, and a chevron in the page's danger red rotates open
  while the answer fades in (the shared reduced-motion rule can
  kill both, as it does every animation on the internal pages).
  Collapsed by default: the warning reads first, the evidence is
  one deliberate click away.
  The matching entry comes from a new public query,
  ThreatBlockManager::matchingListEntry() - and the walk behind it
  is the point of the change: the parent-domain walk that every
  blocking decision uses existed as three inline copies
  (isHostBlocked, shouldBlock, the session-allow check); they are
  now one file-local matchingEntry() helper that answers "which
  entry of this set covers this host", so the entry the page shows
  can never disagree with the decision it explains. The query is a
  pure list query (neither the enabled switch nor a session allow
  changes it - it reports what the list says, not what the
  protection would do), thread safe like every read path, and the
  page reads it under the same lock as the list state, so the
  three rows describe one coherent snapshot of the blocklist,
  never the decision of one refresh mixed with the entries of
  another.
  The rows are honest about the states a warning page can be
  reached in, not just the common one: an interstitial rendered
  with no list loaded says "unknown (the blocklist is not loaded)"
  instead of guessing, one reached for a host the current list no
  longer carries (a stale address reloaded after a refresh, a
  hand-crafted osza: URL) says "not on the current blocklist"
  instead of inventing an entry, and an unconfigured manager still
  names the default source - the row is never empty. Every value
  the page interpolates is HTML-escaped, the source included, and
  the source row carries selectable monospace text (like the
  blocked URL) rather than a link - a security interstitial should
  not carry an easy path away to a third-party site, but the
  address can be copied and visited deliberately.
  New autotests: the query itself (exact, normalized, trailing-dot,
  the closest listed ancestor, an exact listing beating the
  ancestor, the suffix spoofs, the empty host, the empty list, the
  purity under the off switch and a session allow, and the entry
  existing exactly when the host is blocked) and the interstitial
  extended (the disclosure and its question, both match tails, the
  no-current-match and not-loaded honesty, the source row escaping
  a hostile source URL, with the source saved and restored). The
  docs screenshot shows the page with the disclosure open.
  Verified without a Qt build by the new
  tools/security/verify_threat_interstitial.py (75 checks), which
  also pins the contracts: one walk behind every question, the
  placeholder contract on both sides of the page, the native
  disclosure's markup and no-script toggle, the one-snapshot read,
  the escaping discipline, a behavioral simulation of the walk, and
  the deadlock guard (the page reads through the file-local helper
  under its lock, never through the self-locking public query).
- Security tools (18): the protections-relaxed badge in the location
  bar. The per-site state the padlock menu answers for was invisible
  until the menu was opened - a site with an HTTPS-Only exclusion, a
  fingerprint or GPC opt-out exclusion, or a threat-block "proceed
  anyway" paused for the session looked exactly like a fully
  protected one. The site icon now carries a small amber badge
  (drawn into the icon itself, so it follows wherever the icon goes,
  and scaled by the icon area's size, so the popup windows' location
  bar - which paints it over its own icon area through the same one
  drawing code - shows the same badge) whenever the current site has
  such an exception, and its tooltip names the relaxed protections
  by the porter's display names; clicking the icon opens the padlock
  menu, where each tool's row states and changes the site's
  treatment. The badge answers only for per-site choices, and only
  while the tool itself is enabled - a global switch is not per-site
  state, so turning a protection off everywhere retires the badge
  with it, and nothing is badged on the browser's own osza: pages.
  The state is judged in one place, SecurityManager::relaxedProtections()
  (the same four tools, enabled first then the site's own exception,
  exactly the way each padlock-menu row judges), and it refreshes
  everywhere it can move: on every URL change (a same-scheme
  navigation between two sites carries it without a favicon or
  privacy change to help), on load-finished (reloads - the padlock
  menu's per-site toggles land here when they reload the page), on
  settings reload (a global switch or an import retires exceptions),
  when the completer closes after typing (the search icon carries no
  site state, so the badge is cleared while typing), and when the
  padlock menu closes (an HTTPS-Only exception on an https page
  reloads nothing - the one change only this hook can catch, through
  the new SiteIcon::siteInfoClosed signal). New autotests: the query
  itself (each tool joining under its own exception in the padlock
  menu's row order, subdomains covered, a sibling host clean, a tool
  switched off globally retiring the site's exception with it, the
  osza:/file:/empty scheme gate) and the badge end to end in a real
  window's location bar (the composited icon and the tooltip naming
  the tool, the same-scheme navigation to a clean host clearing it,
  the browser's own pages carrying no state). Verified without a Qt
  build by tools/security/verify_locationbar_badge.py.
- Security tools (17): per-site state for the two tools whose state
  was invisible in the padlock menu - the GPC signal and malicious
  site protection. The padlock menu now carries four per-site rows
  (HTTPS-Only mode, fingerprint protection, the GPC signal,
  malicious site protection), each with its own "diff" deep link
  into the recommended baseline's settings-diff page, built by the
  same honest gate the first two rows use. The GPC row states
  whether the opt-out is sent to this site and toggles a per-site
  exclusion: a persisted host list (the same "host and subdomains,
  *.wildcard" matching the fingerprint and HTTPS-Only exceptions
  use) that both halves of the signal honor - the injected script
  leaves navigator.globalPrivacyControl off the excluded host's
  environment, and the network interceptor skips the Sec-GPC
  header for requests whose own target host is excluded, so while
  browsing an excluded site every request to every other host
  still carries the opt-out. The design decision went to
  persistence for GPC (it is a preference about a site, like the
  other per-site exception lists, managed from the padlock menu
  for the current site and from a new GPC exceptions dialog in
  Preferences in bulk) and to the session for threat-block: a
  "proceed anyway" for a host the blocklist calls malicious must
  not survive a restart silently, so the padlock menu's row only
  makes the paused state visible and revocable - "Block this site
  again" ends the session exception now (the new
  ThreatBlockManager::removeSessionAllow, which removes the
  covers-the-subdomains exception the interstitial created), and
  the only way to create one remains the interstitial's explicit
  warning-acknowledging choice. The row is honest about the states
  it cannot act on: the blocklist still loading, a flagged host,
  an unflagged host. The sidebar's GPC and threat-block state
  lines and the self-test page's GPC line follow: both name the
  current site's exclusion or session pause instead of claiming
  "every request". The settings porter's globalPrivacyControl
  section gained the exclusions field (exported from the live
  manager, validated on import, applied through
  GpcManager::setExclusions, and diffed with the sites whose
  treatment changes named - a missing field resets the list, the
  same contract httpsOnly and fingerprintProtection follow), and
  the three presets state the field with an empty list, so a
  preset stays a complete replacement of the configuration. New
  autotests: the GPC exclusion manager (matching, normalization,
  persistence, the script carrying the list, setExclusions as the
  porter's path), the GPC exceptions dialog round trip, the
  end-to-end DOM and header halves on an excluded host (the
  property absent while the signal is on, the header absent to the
  excluded host alone), the threat-block session revoke (the
  exact host, the subdomains, the parent-domain allow revoked from
  a subdomain, the empty host), the porter round trip, preview and
  presets with the new field, and the padlock menu's four diff
  links.
- Security tools (16): the copy-link action on the self-test page's
  sections - the security self-test joins the deep links. Each of the
  page's four parts (the protections snapshot, the live fingerprint
  checks, the noise tiles, the verify-on-a-real-site links) now
  carries its own "#" anchor on its heading, and a plain click on it
  copies that section's address to the clipboard - the "#" becomes a
  checkmark in the accent color while the address sits there, then
  says "#" again - so a single part of the self-test can be pointed
  at, pasted into a note or a bug report, shared or bookmarked.
  Everything else that reads an href keeps its own meaning: modified
  clicks, middle-click, the context menu's "Copy link address",
  bookmarks. The copy prefers the async Clipboard API
  (navigator.clipboard.writeText) and falls back to the legacy
  execCommand path when the API is missing or refuses, exactly like
  the diff page's anchors - on the very clipboard the previous
  release taught the browser to answer for its own pages. The
  address is osza:selftest?section=<id> (the ids: protections,
  fingerprint, noise, verify), and the URL shape exists once:
  SelfTestPage::pageUrl() builds it, sectionIdFromUrl() reads it
  back, the scheme handler hands the requested section to the page,
  and the page's own script spells the same shape for its anchors -
  the same contract the settings-diff page's deep links follow. A
  request asking for a section lands focused on it: marked,
  scrolled to, introduced by a note that names the section by its
  own heading; an address naming a section the page does not have
  is answered with a note over the whole page, everything shown
  from the top, never an error - which sections exist is the page's
  judgment, and the browser stays a pipe for the one value. The
  offline verifier for the page grew from 29 to 76 checks: the URL
  shape is pinned on all four sides (pageUrl(), sectionIdFromUrl(),
  the scheme handler, the page's own anchor builder), and the copy
  action and the landing run in the mocked DOM on every path they
  can take - the async copy, the refused-then-legacy copy, nothing
  copying at all, the double click appending its state once, the
  modified click left to the browser, the focused and the unknown
  section. A new autotest pins the URL helpers (the shapes, the
  trimming, the round trip, the verbatim pass-through of an unknown
  id) and the real page through the scheme handler: the four
  anchors carry the pageUrl() addresses, the focused section is
  marked and noted, the unknown one is answered.
- Security tools (15): the copy-link action on the diff page's
  section anchors, and the padlock menu's per-site entries join the
  deep links. The "#" anchor of a tool's section carried the URL
  that opens the diff focused there, but taking that address
  anywhere meant reading it out of a tooltip-less hover or the
  context menu. A plain click on the anchor now copies the deep link
  to the clipboard - the "#" becomes a checkmark in the accent color
  while the address sits there, then says "#" again - and everything
  else that reads an href keeps its own meaning: modified clicks,
  middle-click, the context menu's "Copy link address", bookmarks.
  When nothing can be copied the anchor says so in its title, and
  the context menu's way still works. The copy prefers the async
  Clipboard API (navigator.clipboard.writeText) and falls back to
  the legacy execCommand path when the API is missing or refuses,
  so the anchor works on every Qt WebEngine Osza runs on. The
  clipboard itself needed a look: the async API asks for a feature
  permission, and the HTML5 permission manager has no site-settings
  row for it - on the browser's own osza: pages the request would
  warn about an unknown feature and never be answered, leaving the
  page's promise pending. WebPage::featurePermissionRequested now
  answers the clipboard features for osza: origins: a sanitized
  write is granted (the same thing the legacy path already allows
  for internal pages), a clipboard read is denied - matching
  JavascriptCanPaste, which stays off for them - and before Qt 6.6,
  where the two are one combined feature that the writes arrive as,
  it is granted, the pages being the browser's own either way.
  Every other feature still goes through the permission manager.
  The padlock menu's per-site entries (HTTPS-Only mode, fingerprint
  protection) carry the same "diff" link the dashboard's rows do -
  the answer to "what would the recommended setting for this tool
  be?" one click away from the per-site state - and the landing is
  shared now: SettingsDiffPage::recommendedBaseline() resolves the
  baseline through the preset catalog in the one place, and
  SettingsDiffPage::openToolDiff() decides where a tool key lands
  (the focused diff, or the baselines overview when the request
  cannot be answered honestly - never an error page), with the
  dashboard's slot delegating to it, so the two can never drift
  apart. The links hide with their rows on the browser's own pages,
  exactly like the entries they belong to. The offline verifier grew
  from 70 to 111 checks: the URL shape is pinned on five sides now -
  the page's links, pageUrl(), the scheme handler, the dashboard's
  rows and the padlock's entries, all through the one resolver and
  the one shared landing - and the copy action runs in the mocked
  DOM on every path it can take (the async copy, the refused-then-
  legacy copy, nothing copying at all, the modified click left to
  the browser), with the clipboard policy of webpage.cpp pinned
  statically: the grant, the deny, the Qt 6.6 split, older Qt's
  combined feature. A new autotest pins the padlock's side: exactly
  the two per-site tools carry a link, each showing the pageUrl()
  address of the recommended baseline focused on its tool, each
  tooltip naming the baseline and the tool, external links off,
  hidden with the rows on the browser's own pages - and the shared
  landing, with a real window, opens the very address the link
  shows, trims whitespace around a key, answers an unknown key with
  the overview and is a no-op without a window.
- Security tools (14): the dashboard's per-tool rows link to their
  part of the recommended baseline's diff. The osza:settings-diff
  page answers a "tool" query item and the import flow links to it,
  but the security dashboard - the one place every tool lives as a
  row with its state and its quick switch - could not reach the diff
  of a single tool. Every protection row now carries a "diff" link
  beside its quick switch, and following it opens the recommended
